    "available_at": "2026-11-23T16:39:26.123679+00:00",
    "body": "By Stuart Todd\n23 November 2026\nAn EU cap on imports of Chinese hybrid cars could deal a serious blow to the business of European finished vehicle logistics (FVL) players, given this is one the most dynamic segments of the market at present.\nHowever, while not playing down the impact of a move designed to protect the bloc ’ s incumbent OEMs, the Brussels-based Association of European Vehicle Logistics (ECG remains upbeat on the sector ’ s flexibility and ability to adapt.\nThe EU plans a cap on imports of Chinese hybrid vehicles of 350,000 units, from 1 December, after Beijing agreed to curb exports in a 16-point trade consensus.\nThere is provision to increase the cap by 6% annually thereafter.\nAccording to Eurostat, the EU ’ s official data source, in the first eight months of 2026. imports of hybrid cars had surpassed the full-year 2025 volume by 64%, and reached 474,475 units – a 175% year-on-year jump.\n“ It ’ s not our role to say how many vehicles should be imported. Our interest is in understanding how measures like this could change vehicle flows and what that would mean for logistics capacity,” ECG executive director Frank Schnelle told The Loadstar .\n“ Limiting imports to 350,000 units annually would clearly affect FVL operators that handle a lot of hybrid vehicles from China. But a reduction in such imports would not automatically mean fewer cars being sold in Europe. If overall demand holds up, some of that volume could be replaced by other types of automobiles, including battery electric vehicles from China. or vehicles sourced from other markets.\n“ For our members, this could mean a shift in transport routes and where vehicles are handled. Capacity is currently well-used overall. Ocean shipping lines are ordering vessels and vehicle storage areas are being expanded at ports.”\nThe surge in Chinese hybrid cars into the EU over the past couple of years was largely the result of hefty anti -subsidy tariffs on BEVs from China in October 2024, explained Rico Luman, senior sector economist, transport & logistics, at Dutch bank ING.\n“ Basically, Chinese brands shifted their sales efforts to hybrids to sidestep these tariffs ,” he said.\nHowever, the tariffs have done little to stop Chinese EV growth in Europe, and sales have remained resilient despite the duties.\nMr Luman quoted data showing China’s share of European new car sales has continued to grow, reaching 10% for the first nine months of the year, compared with 6% in the corresponding period last year.\n“ I would expect (in light of the cap on hybrids) a renewed push for Chinese BEV vehicles. Also, a brand like BYD which plans to start its own production in Szeged, in Hungary, will likely start turning out hybrids for the European market, alongside full-electric vehicles. Others, such as Leapmotor, could do this too.\n“ All -in-all, this supports European production and will temper Chinese imports somewhat, but without stalling import growth completely. European FVL players will see a reshaping of trade routes. New production locations and ‘ co – operative ’ locations (between OEMs) will emerge and be scale d up, requiring parts that will need to be sourced from Europe. In a changing market landscape agility will be paramount.”\nThe latest figures from Europe’s biggest port for finished vehicle traffic, Antwerp-Zeebrugge, showed the Far East, which includes China, accounted for more than 55% of imports – 706,228 units – in the first nine months up 28% year on year.\n“ The port is operating at near-maximum capacity, given the huge imports from China, among others, ” a spokesperson added.",
